17 lower-48 test floods from 2017-2025 were studied, comprising 15 STN events with ≥50 surveyed, non-coastal high-water marks and two NOAA-only events with ≥10 direct flood deaths; seven development floods were used to develop and check methods. I expanded the high-water-mark locations by 0.25° and used NOAA flood-record coordinates for the NOAA-only events. I utilized documented STN or NOAA event dates with one day of padding on each side.
Continuous gage height data (parameter 00065, measured in feet) was retrieved from USGS. I retained successful stream-site records, producing 6,783 gauge-event records covering 4,062 distinct gauges. I collected drainage area, location, equipment limits, flood stage, and prior-record information and matched USGS gauges to NWS station IDs. Mapping uncertainties were retained. IEM’s archived HADS stage readings were downloaded for corresponding stations and event windows. USGS gage-height retrievals were logged on November 26-27, 2026, with saved-file timestamps of November 26 for STN/NOAA event records and November 27 for station metadata, the NWS crosswalk, and IEM observations.
For archive gaps, USGS qualifiers were normalized. Estimated and apparently inserted readings were separated from regular observations. Archive-dark gaps lasted at least max(60 minutes, 3.5 times the median reporting interval) and followed a >0.3-ft rise in 30 minutes, covered the peak, or began after reaching halfway between the event minimum and maximum and before the peak. Open-ended gaps required a rise-period or peak overlap.
The reference crest was the maximum USGS archive stage within the event window. I separately scanned live series for gaps and classified behaviors within archive gaps as SILENT (<20% coverage), FROZEN (≤0.10-ft range for ≥2 hours, ≥0.5 ft below the crest), UNDER_READ (varying readings peaking >2 ft below the crest), IMPLAUSIBLE (error values, negatives, or readings ≥10 ft above the crest), or otherwise LIVE_PRESENT. Absent station IDs or numeric live records were NO_LIVE_DATA. Ambiguous mappings or missing files were UNRESOLVED. As multiple patterns can occur within one gap, classification priority went from dominant FROZEN (qualifying plateau covering ≥50% of gap’s readings) , SILENT, IMPLAUSIBLE, UNDER_READ, other FROZEN, then LIVE_PRESENT. Each gap received the first applicable classification.
Network outages were screened using an 80% simultaneous-silence threshold. Validation included five USGS maximum-stage comparisons (seed 43), 36 hand-labeled pilot cases to develop classifying rules, ten census plots (seed 47), and missing-record rechecks, with disagreements documented.
